d) $\displaystyle w = -\frac{\mu - \mu_0}{2g} n^2 w (x_2 - x_1)(i_2^2 - i_1^2)$

for an electrically linear system with negligible losses, the source work over one full cycle is the area enclosed in the $\lambda$-$i$ plane:

\[
w_{\rm src}=\oint i\,d\lambda
\]

the flux linkage is $\lambda=n\phi$, and magnetic circuit analysis gives

\[
\phi
=
\frac{n i}
{
\dfrac{g}{\mu_0 d w}
+
\dfrac{x}{\mu_0 w g}
+
\dfrac{d-x}{\mu w g}
}
\]

for $d\ll l$, the $d$-dependent yoke reluctance is negligible compared with the large block reluctance, so approximately

\[
\lambda(x,i)
=
\frac{n^2 i}
{
\dfrac{1}{\mu_0 d}
+
\dfrac{x}{\mu_0 g}
+
\dfrac{l-x}{\mu g}
}
\]

thus $\lambda$ is proportional to $i$ at fixed $x$, but depends nonlinearly on $x$. evaluating the rectangular path gives

\[
w_{\rm src}
=
(i_2-i_1)\left[\lambda(x_1)-\lambda(x_2)\right]
-
\int_{x_1}^{x_2}\left[\lambda(x,i_2)-\lambda(x,i_1)\right]dx
\]

after integration and simplification, the leading result is

\[
w_{\rm src}
=
-\frac{\mu-\mu_0}{2g}
n^2 w (x_2-x_1)(i_2^2-i_1^2)
\]

therefore the correct choice is d.

